Trustworthy AI involves the development and deployment of systems that are robust, lawful, and ethical, guided by principles such as transparency, fairness, safety, accountability, and privacy~\cite{cabrero-danielPerceivedTrustworthinessNatural2023, jakeschHowDifferentGroups2022, jobinGlobalLandscapeAI2019, ozmengaribaySixHumanCenteredArtificial2023}. From a human-centered perspective, this means ensuring that AI systems align with human values, safety, agency, and dignity throughout their lifecycle. Higher user trustworthiness perceptions in generative AI systems are beneficial and correlate with greater use and actual adoption~\cite{cabrero-danielPerceivedTrustworthinessNatural2023, tolsdorfSafetyPerceptionsGenerative2025, choudhuryInvestigatingImpactUser2023}.
User trustworthiness perceptions in generative AI are shaped by multiple factors~\cite{balaynUnpackingTrustDynamics2025}, such as the context of use~\cite{folstadUsersExperiencesChatbots2020}, the user's state of mind or the support they are looking for~\cite{wangFactorsInfluencingPerceived2020}, the level of perceived control~\cite{liuPrevalenceOvershadowsConcerns2024}, as well as users' personal backgrounds and experiences~\cite{cabrero-danielPerceivedTrustworthinessNatural2023, harringtonTrustComfortRelatability2023, kieslichMyFutureMy2024}. 
Trust in AI can form rapidly based on first impressions and perceived competence~\cite{ngTrustAIChatbots2025}, and users exposed to early errors or bias report lower trust than those who encounter such issues later in the interaction~\cite{amaroBelieveArtificialIntelligence2023}.%\mahmood{say something about how our work is different?}

block-7d773810e6017b3d.raw:
Further, participants audited for outputs that could have social or moral consequences. \textbf{Unfair demographic attribution \& prejudice}~($n=62$) reflects the goal to probe for stereotypes or biased attributions related to race, gender, and other identities.
\textbf{Harmful or offensive}~($n=47$) captures participants' anticipation that the chatbot might produce inappropriate, disrespectful, or dangerous responses. Participants would look for outputs that might include \textit{``responses that lacked empathy or were disrespectful,''} or include \textit{``awful things about immigrants.''} Others sought to determine \textit{``if the chatbot would give good and harmless advice to a medical question.''} %aimed to explore whether the model would mishandle sensitive contexts, such as \textit{``seeking medical/mental advice.''}
\textbf{Ethical breaches}~($n=13$) capture outputs with a strong emphasis on normative correctness or morally questionable content, which may or may not cause immediate harm. Participants expected the chatbot might generate \textit{``problematic outputs that went against academic integrity and moral standards''} or \textit{``encourage the user to continue with illegal activities.''}

block-c034567f92233e03.raw:
\textbf{Complete and correct information}~($n=96$) captures participants noting the completeness of information provided, referring to the level of detail and breadth of perspectives and viewpoints captured in the outputs:
\textit{``I feel the [chatbot] had more information than I was aware of. I liked the fact of how thorough and detailed the chatbot was. I learned something I did not know.''} 
The accuracy of information was also frequently mentioned, although participants may not cross-validate the information provided. As one participant stated: \textit{``The chatbot recited what seemed like accurate statistics and offered solutions that were fair.''}

block-01a46077d06801f6.raw:
\textbf{Perceived neutrality and absence of subjectivity}~($n=65$) relates broadly to the neutrality and impartiality of the chatbot in its responses. Neutrality was generally noted as the chatbot providing primarily factual or objective information. As one participant noted: 
\textit{``I didn't really find any problematic outputs from these responses. More so, they were objective and nuanced.''} 
Other participants saw neutrality as remaining impartial even when discussing highly contentious topics:
\textit{``I believe based on the information provided it kept an unbiased opinion on a hot button issue.''}

block-1a4d988d4990099d.raw:
\textbf{Good communication skills}~($n=32$) captures participants' feedback on the chatbot's ability to clearly and understandably communicate in its outputs.
In particular, they mentioned the clarity of the outputs as being important: \textit{``I found that the chatbot was not problematic. It seemed to fairly access the information I requested and answered in a way I could understand.''}
Some participants also characterized the chatbot's reasoning (when given) as understandable and sound:
\textit{``The responses were fair, balanced, well-reasoned, appropriate in tone and context and morally just.''}

